  "result": "Order states are checked in memory and then written to a JSON file. Nothing in the app connects the two steps.\n\n**Validation: `shop/orders.py`**\n- `STATES` (line 6) lists the states: `new`, `paid`, `shipped`, `cancelled`.\n- `ALLOWED` (line 7) maps each state to the states it can move to: `new` \u2192 `paid` or `cancelled`, `paid` \u2192 `shipped` or `cancelled`. `shipped` and `cancelled` are final.\n- `Order.__init__` starts every order at `\"new\"` (line 17).\n- `Order.move(state)` (lines 19\u201322) raises `ValueError` if the move isn't in `ALLOWED[self.state]`. Otherwise it sets `self.state`.\n\n**Storage: `shop/storage.py`**\n- `Store.save_order(order)` (lines 16\u201321) loads every row with `Store.load()` and drops the row with the same `id`. It then appends the order's id, customer, total, tax and state, and rewrites the whole file with `Path.write_text`.\n\n**Gaps**\n- `save_order` doesn't validate anything. It saves whatever `order.state` holds.\n- `state` is a plain public attribute, so `order.state = \"shipped\"` skips `move` entirely.\n- `move()` is only called in `tests/test_orders.py:20\u201323`, and nothing calls `save_order` at all. No code path moves an order and then saves it.\n\nToken Beggar: routed 1 subtask(s) to haiku",
